<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marion Mahony Griffin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1871">1871</a>–<a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marion Mahony Griffin was an American architect and artist, among the first women licensed as architects in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and an early member of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frank-lloyd-wright">Frank Lloyd Wright</a>'s studio. Her striking drawings presented the plan for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canberra">Canberra</a> with which her husband and partner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-burley-griffin">Walter Burley Griffin</a> won the international competition in 1912. She worked with him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marion-mahony-griffin.jpg" alt="Marion Mahony Griffin" width="116" height="148" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Walterburleygriffin_(cropped)2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Marion Lucy Mahony was born in Chicago, the daughter of Jeremiah Mahony, a journalist, poet and teacher from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a>, and Clara Hamilton, a schoolteacher. After the Great Chicago Fire the family moved to Winnetka, whose open landscape later shaped her attention to nature. Following her father's suicide in 1882 her mother became a school principal in Chicago and was active in women's groups. Encouraged by her cousin, the architect Dwight Perkins, and funded by Anna Wilmarth, she studied architecture at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, graduating in 1894 as the second woman to do so after Sophia Hayden.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>She began in Perkins's office in Steinway Hall and in 1898 became the first licensed female architect in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>. Frank Lloyd Wright hired her as his first employee in 1895, and she worked with him until 1909, designing buildings, furniture, stained glass and decorative panels. Her drawings, which combined perspective, plan and section on one sheet and drew on Japanese prints, became a mark of his style; historians now credit her with about half the drawings in his Wasmuth Portfolio, though Wright gave her little credit. When he left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1909, she worked under Hermann V. von Holst on his abandoned commissions.</p><p>She married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-burley-griffin">Walter Burley Griffin</a> in 1911 and worked in his practice. Thomas S. Hines and other historians hold that her watercolour perspectives were instrumental in winning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canberra">Canberra</a> competition. From 1914 she ran the couple's Sydney office and its private commissions, among them Cafe Australia, Newman College and the Capitol Theatre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-griffins">Griffins</a> embraced Rudolf Steiner's anthroposophy, developed the Knitlock building method and settled at Castlecrag. In 1936 she joined her husband's practice in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a>, managing the office and training draughtsmen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Walter's death she returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1939 and largely retired. She spent about twenty years on The Magic of America, a 1,400-page account with 650 illustrations, never formally published but online since 2007. Suffering memory loss, she died in poverty in 1961 aged 90 and was buried in Graceland Cemetery. Long treated as a helper of Wright and her husband, she was later described by Reyner Banham as the first woman architect who needed no apology in a world of men. A Chicago beach was named after her in 2015.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marion Mahony Griffin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The plan for Canberra came from an international competition won in 1912 by the American architect Walter Burley Griffin, with drawings by his wife and partner <b>Marion Mahony Griffin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Chicago, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Chicago, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.88,-87.63&amp;year=1929" aria-label="Chicago on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2419 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Marion Mahony Griffin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2869 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2869 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.88,-87.63&amp;year=1929">What was here in 1929? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
